  "query_preview": "..(This Evening Through Thursday)\nIssued at 130 PM MST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nCurrent upper air models and Water Vapor show upper ridge axis\nover the Dakotas this afternoon, with upper trough over the west\ncoast and Rockies region. Surface analysis shows high pressure\ncentered over South Dakota, and a surface low over southwestern\nWyoming. Satellite has broken to overcast mid/high clouds across\nthe forecast area. Temperatures currently sit in the 40s to low\n50s, with light winds out of the southeast. \n\nFor tonight and overnight, light southeasterly winds could promote\npatchy areas of fog early Friday morning over the western SD \nplains, but cloud cover still in the region will keep any fog \nisolated. Surface low and associated cold front slides up into the\nregion during the day Friday. Gusty west-northwest winds are \nexpected behind the boundary 25-35 mph. Models show some light \nprecipitation moving into the region post-front, but limited \nmoisture as these showers move into drier air. General consensus \nkeeps rain/snow showers limited to northeastern WY into the Black \nHills and northwestern SD, with QPF amounts of 0.1\" or less Friday\nafternoon through Saturday morning. \n\nBy Saturday, cyclonic flow aloft sets up over the region,\npromoting temperatures 5-10F below normal through early next week.\nLonger range models show potential for a shortwave Tues-Weds of\nnext week, bringing another chance for precipitation, however\ncurrently the better forcing is well south of the forecast area.\nMild upper ridging sets up over the Rockies again by Wednesday,\nand we'll see a gradual increase in temperatures middle of next\nweek.",
